## The energetic price

The wall model uses $\rho=-\kappa v^2(f_y^2+f_z^2)$. Explore how its negative sign and quadratic scaling follow from a squared transverse gradient. For wider context, see [energy conditions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_condition).

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the sign and scaling proof</summary>

When $\kappa\ge0$, every factor after the minus sign is nonnegative, hence $\rho\le0$. If $\kappa>0$, $v\ne0$, and the transverse gradient is nonzero, the inequality is strict. Replacing $v$ by $av$ multiplies both pointwise and sampled energy by $a^2$.

</details>

